# {{ projectName }}

Built with the **api** starter kit — a JSON API with bearer-token
authentication, CORS, rate limiting, OpenAPI annotations and Drizzle. No view
layer.

## Run it

```bash
deno task dev      # http://localhost:8888
deno task test     # the smoke tests — no database needed
```

`GET /` answers immediately. Everything else needs a database.

## Add the database

The scaffold boots without one on purpose: `DATABASE_URL` is commented out in
`.env`, so the kernel skips the connection instead of printing a failure on
every reload. When you are ready:

```bash
# .env
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/{{ projectName }}
```

```bash
deno task db:migrate
```

Then create a user. The `password` column holds a **hash** — never a password:

```ts
import { hashPassword } from '@lockness/auth'
await db.insert(users).values({
    email: 'you@example.com',
    password: await hashPassword('correct horse battery staple'),
})
```

## Get a token, use a token

```bash
curl -X POST localhost:8888/auth/token \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"email":"you@example.com","password":"correct horse battery staple"}'
# → { "token": "...", "type": "Bearer" }

curl localhost:8888/auth/me -H 'Authorization: Bearer <token>'
# → { "user": { "id": 1, "email": "you@example.com", "name": null } }
```

The plaintext token is returned **once**. Only its hash is stored, so a dump of
`access_tokens` is not a set of working credentials — and a lost token is
reissued, not recovered.

## What is here

```text
app/
├── auth/
│   ├── guards.ts         # the 'api' TokenGuard
│   └── user_provider.ts  # the two lookups the provider needs
├── controller/
│   ├── health_controller.ts  # GET /
│   └── token_controller.ts   # POST /auth/token, GET /auth/me
├── model/user.ts         # users + access_tokens
├── kernel.ts             # CORS, auth, database, cache
└── routes.ts             # explicit registry, for production
config/                   # app, cache, compile, database, listeners
database/migrations/      # 0000_create_users.sql
tests/smoke.test.ts
```

## Rate limiting

Routes carry a budget through decorators from `@lockness/core`:

| Decorator             | Budget          | Use for                     |
| :-------------------- | :-------------- | :-------------------------- |
| `@ThrottleApi()`      | 100 / minute    | ordinary endpoints          |
| `@ThrottleLogin()`    | strict          | credential exchange         |
| `@ThrottleSensitive()`| strict          | password reset, 2FA         |
| `@ThrottleHeavy()`    | 10 / minute     | reports, exports, uploads   |
| `@Throttle(n, '1m')`  | whatever you say| anything else               |

`POST /auth/token` uses `@ThrottleLogin()` rather than the API budget, because
that is the endpoint someone brute-forces.

## CORS

`CORS_ORIGIN` in `.env`, comma-separated, `*` by default so the scaffold works
from any front end on day one. **Narrow it before you ship.** With credentials
a browser refuses `*` outright; without them it still means any page on the
internet can read your responses.

## OpenAPI

Routes are annotated with `@ApiDoc` from `@lockness/openapi`. To serve the
document and a Swagger UI, add a controller:

```ts
import { generateOpenAPISpec, serveSwaggerUI } from '@lockness/openapi'

@Controller('/docs')
export class DocsController {
    @Get('/openapi.json')
    spec(c: Context) {
        return c.json(generateOpenAPISpec(controllers, { title: '{{ projectName }}' }))
    }
}
```

## What is deliberately missing

No JSX, no Tailwind, no session. A JSON API that authenticates with a cookie is
a CSRF surface nobody asked for; a bearer token is the whole of the request
state here.

Want views? Scaffold the web kit instead:

```bash
deno run -A jsr:@lockness/init my-app --kit=web
```

## Commands

```bash
deno task cli make:controller Product
deno task cli make:model Product -a
deno task cli router:list
deno task cli db:migrate
deno task routes:generate
```

